{"qid:Q15708315":{"key":"qid:Q15708315","title":"Guselkumab","compound":{"key":"qid:Q15708315","cid":null,"qid":"Q15708315","title":"guselkumab","iupac":null,"wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guselkumab","cas":"1350289-85-8","iuphar":"7998","drugbank":"DB11834","chemspider":null,"unii":"089658A12D","kegg":"D10438","chebi":null,"chembl":null,"pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":null,"echa":null,"description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"L04AC16","mesh":null,"uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:26+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":null,"chembl_phase":null,"first_approval":null,"bbw":null,"fda":null,"ema":null,"control":null,"reg_at":null},"status":null,"pharm":[],"safety":[],"manufacturing":[],"structure":false,"populations":{"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":4,"forms":[["Solution for Subcutaneous Injection",1],["Concentrate For Solution For I.V Infusion",1],["Solution for Infusion",1],["Solution in prefilled pen",1]]}},"reg":{"fda":null,"ema":null,"phase":null,"first_approval":null,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":false,"aware":null},"chem":null,"eml":false,"names":[["guselkumab",4]],"atc":[{"code":"L04AC16","path":[{"code":"L","label":"Antineoplastic and immunomodulating agents"},{"code":"L04","label":"Immunosuppressants"},{"code":"L04A","label":"Immunosuppressive drug"},{"code":"L04AC","label":null},{"code":"L04AC16","label":null}]}],"by_type":{"biological":4},"co_ingredients":[]},"cid:13144":{"key":"cid:13144","title":"Calcium lactate","compound":{"key":"cid:13144","cid":13144,"qid":"Q419693","title":"Calcium Lactate","iupac":"calcium bis(2-hydroxypropanoate)","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcium_lactate","cas":"814-80-2","iuphar":null,"drugbank":"DB13231","chemspider":"12592","unii":"2URQ2N32W3","kegg":null,"chebi":null,"chembl":"CHEMBL2106111","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID0020236","echa":"100.011.278","description":"group of stereoisomers","kind":null,"atc":"A12AA05","mesh":null,"uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:45+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"CC(C(=O)[O-])O.CC(C(=O)[O-])O.[Ca+2]","chembl_phase":1.0,"first_approval":null,"bbw":0,"fda":0,"ema":0,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:52:19+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"Indicated for use as the nutritional supplement.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","items":[{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"EXPTL USE: ORAL ADMIN OF CALCIUM LACTATE (7.7 G) LOWERED PLASMA PARATHYROID HORMONE BY 40-43% & DECR URINARY EXCRETION OF CYCLIC ADENOSINE MONOPHOSPHATE BY 30-54% IN SKELETAL-TYPE HYPERPARATHYROIDISM BUT NOT IN OTHER TYPES OR IN CONTROLS.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"EXPTL USE: HIGH DOSES OF ORAL CALCIUM LACTATE DECR URINARY CYCLIC ADENOSINE MONOPHOSPHATE EXCRETION IN BORDERLINE CASES OF UROLITHIC AND CHEM HYPERPARATHYROIDISM.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"May be used to treat mild hypocalcemia and for maintenance therapy. /From table/","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"},{"section":"Therapeutic Uses","text":"MEDICATION (VET): IN CALCIUM THERAPY","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Both components of calcium lactate, calcium ion and lactic acid, play essential roles in the human body as a skeletal element an energy source, respectively.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"In aqueous environments such as the gastrointestinal (GI) tract, calcium lactate will dissociate into calcium cation and lactic acid anions, the conjugate base of lactic acid. Lactic acid is a naturally-occurring compound that serves as fuel or energy in mammals by acting as an ubiquitous intermediate in the metabolic pathways. Lactic acid diffuses through the muscles and is transported to the liver by the bloodstream to participate in gluconeogenesis.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"In order to be absorbed, calcium must be in its freely soluble form (Ca2+) or bound to a soluble organic molecule. Calcium absorption mainly occurs at the duodenum and proximal jejunum due to more acidic pH and the abundance of the calcium binding proteins. The mean calcium absorption is about 25% of calcium intake (range is 10 – 40%) in the small intestine, and is mediated by both passive diffusion and active transport.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Following oral administration to a human volunteer, 20 to 30% of a dose of lactic acid of up to 3000 mg was excreted via the urine during a period of 14 hours.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"The majority of calcium absorbed (99%) is stored in the skeleton and teeth for structural integrity.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"No pharmacokinetic data available.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"No pharmacokinetic data available.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"In hepatic gluconeogenesis, lactic acid is converted to glucose. Lactic acid may be further catabolyzed in the lactic acid cycle.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"RUMINAL INGESTA FROM COWS FED 2.5 L GRAIN-ALFALFA HAY MIXT PROVIDING 545 G OF SODIUM LACTATE & CALCIUM LACTATE DAILY INCUBATED WITH SODIUM LACTATE OR 17 POLY LACTIC ACID. ACETATE WAS PRIMARY END PRODUCT BUT OXIDN OF LACTATE CAUSED SYNTH OF BUTYRATE FROM ACETATE.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/web_policies.html"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"No pharmacokinetic data available.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB13231","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Interactions","items":[{"section":"Interactions","text":"IN TREATMENT OF TETANY, ITS ABSORPTION IS APPARENTLY ENHANCED BY SIMULTANEOUS ADMIN OF LACTOSE.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976"}]},{"section":"Toxicity Summary","items":[{"section":"Toxicity Summary","text":"Based on the available information included in this report, the CIR Expert Panel concludes that Glycolic and Lactic Acid, their common salts and their simple esters, are safe for use in cosmetic products at concentrations less than or equal to 10%, at final formulation pH greater than or equal to 3.5, when formulated to avoid increasing sun sensitivity or when directions for use include the daily use of sun protection. These ingredients are safe for use in salon products at concentrations less than or equal to 30%, at final formulation pH greater than or equal to 3.0, in products designed for brief, discontinuous use followed by thorough rinsing from the skin, when applied by trained professionals, and when application is accompanied by directions for the daily use of sun protection...Calcium Lactate...","source":"Cosmetic Ingredient Review (CIR)","url":"https://cir-reports.cir-safety.org/cir-ingredient-status-report/?id=09f63e9d-9778-ec11-8d21-000d3a991547"},{"section":"Toxicity Summary","text":"Safe for use in cosmetics, with qualifications","source":"Cosmetic Ingredient Review (CIR)","url":"https://cir-reports.cir-safety.org/cir-ingredient-status-report/?id=09f63e9d-9778-ec11-8d21-000d3a991547"}]}],"manufacturing":[{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","items":[{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","text":"FERMENTATION OF CARBOHYDRATES TO LACTIC ACID, WITH PH CONTROLLED BY CALCIUM CARBONATE; REACTION OF LACTIC ACID WITH CALCIUM CARBONATE OR CALCIUM HYDROXIDE","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976"},{"section":"Methods of Manufacturing","text":"... Commercially by neutralization of lactic acid, from fermentation of dextrose, molasses, starch, sugar or whey, with calcium carbonate.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976"}]},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","items":[{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"CALCIUM LACTATE USP; POWDER USP, TABLETS USP, 325 MG (42.25 MG CALCIUM), & 650 MG (84.5 MG CALCIUM)","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976"},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"Commercial prepn usually contains about 25% water, and on the anhydrous basis it is at least 98% pure.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976"},{"section":"Formulations/Preparations","text":"GRADES: NF; FCC.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976"}]},{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","items":[{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","text":"Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing and Hunting Food, beverage, and tobacco product manufacturing","source":"EPA Chemical Data Reporting (CDR)","url":"https://www.epa.gov/chemical-data-reporting"},{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","text":"Propanoic acid, 2-hydroxy-, calcium salt (2:1): ACTIVE","source":"EPA Chemicals under the TSCA","url":"https://www.epa.gov/chemicals-under-tsca"},{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","text":"Commercial baking powder contains 2.5% calcium lactate.","source":"Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB)","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/source/hsdb/976"}]}],"structure":true,"populations":{},"reg":{"fda":0,"ema":0,"phase":1.0,"first_approval":null,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:13144","title":"Calcium lactate","products":1,"mw":89.1,"logp":-1.88,"tpsa":60.4,"hbd":1,"hba":3,"rotb":1,"rings":0,"heavy":6,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C3H5O3-"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:23666456","title":"Sodium lactate","products":19,"mw":89.1,"logp":-1.88,